 have to go to margaret to  the dutch prime minister and say it is  in your own interest  if you help saving the european sounds  because if the eu falls apart  the dutch will be will be falling apart  too their economic  the economy will fail and and so she  will have to sort of sell this idea to  everybody  and she will just have to make very  clever and very smart little compromise  bits and pieces of compromise that the  people can take home and say  yes we got away relatively well out of  this big struggle  uh for the money in in brussels and  she will in the end be able to do it  because  it is not really on the horizon that one  of the 27 member states  will in the end say and no we'll say no  to this we walk away  it's it has never happened before but  it is going to be super super difficult  of course the other side of the  consensus coin  is a compromise as you were alluding to  barbara  nina uh angela merkel herself  crossed a red line didn't she when she  along with  emmanuel macron agreed to the concept of  joint debt  in the 500 billion euro package that  france and germany  are presenting how has that gone down  domestically  here in germany  you're right that is absolutely the case  she was long known as madame no  in france because of course the french  put a similar proposal on the table  years ago asking for the eu to issue  joint bonds so that money can be  generated that can be given to member  states and angela merkel always refused  that  debt pooling she said it needed to stay  in the fiscal discipline um field and  she was very rigorous when it came to  that topic  then she is somebody who is known to act  when disaster is looming  and she clearly understands that this is  an existential crisis and if  revolutionary and drastic measures  aren't taken this could go to the core  the very core of the european union she  does understand that which is why in  mid-may  she and the french president came  forward and put  this agreement on the table where they  said 500 billion euros  should be issued by the european  commission as  joint bonds on the market and that that  money could then be given as grants  to the countries that are hardest hit  like italy and spain as barbara just  described  and and not as loans and this is the big  controversy but this is also what some  people are calling remarkable  but i hinted at it in my first answer  that of course the german  members of parliament the german leaders  of the industry here they all  understand that if the european  economies around germany  fail if this is a crisis where the  countries don't get out of recession  then this is something that will affect  germany directly  seeing that germany is an export nation  so this is very much in germany's  self-interest but for a long time  she was madame no and she has clearly  changed her mind and people say it's  revolutionary and now with germany  taking over the  rotating presidency of the european  union clearly there's  expectations all across europe in  keeping with that  we asked some of our other  correspondents elsewhere in europe and  around the world about expectations for  germany's  eu presidency here's what people had to  say  since angela merkel took power in 2005  the uk has seen  five different prime ministers and that  is what the brits appreciate about the  german chancellor her endurance her  political skill  and her pragmatism her leadership was  greatly admired during the pandemic when  journalists repeatedly asked downing  street why great britain  wouldn't just follow germany's example  mackel is also seen as pulling the  strings in the eu  now the uk government is hoping that  mackel brings her pragmatism  to the brexit negotiating table to  secure a deal with the brits  germany is considered a reliable  development partner and when germany  took over the g20 presidency  mackel laid a strong focus on africa one  thing kenya will probably take a good  look at  is germany's role at the 